//
// LEGAL POSITIONING NOTE — READ BEFORE EDITING (STRICTEST TIER):
// Greece belongs with the Netherlands in our strictest positioning
// tier. Under Law 1566/1985 (Art. 2 § 3), SCHOOL ATTENDANCE is
// compulsory for children RESIDENT in Greece from age 4 to 15 (two
// compulsory kindergarten years, dimotiko, gymnasio). Parental-choice
// homeschooling is NOT lawful for resident children, and foreign
// online or distance programmes are NOT recognised as fulfilling the
// obligation. The single statutory exception (Art. 32 § 4) is
// kat' oikon didaskalia — home teaching for children whose health or
// special educational needs make attendance especially difficult —
// which is state-administered under the school's supervision. We
// state it factually ONLY; we NEVER position it as a pathway and
// NEVER advise families toward it.
// RESIDENCY, NOT NATIONALITY, IS THE KEY DISTINCTION: families who
// are not resident in Greece and remain legally registered elsewhere
// follow their country of residence's framework. We state this
// precisely and never blur it — a family's residency status is
// theirs to determine, with their own advisers.
// NEVER market homeschooling to Greece-resident families for the
// compulsory years (4-15). The three lawful legs of the Greek offer:
// 1. SUPPLEMENTARY alongside enrolment — the core. And Greece is
//    culturally the easiest supplementary market in our coverage:
//    the FRONTISTIRIO tradition means Greek families already pay for
//    structured after-school tuition as a normal part of education.
//    Smartious supplementary = culturally native.
// 2. THE POST-15 WINDOW — the central wedge. Compulsory education
//    ends after gymnasio (~15); LYKEIO IS OPTIONAL by law. The full
//    A-Level years (15-18) sit entirely outside compulsory
//    education: a Greece-resident teenager may lawfully study
//    full-time live Cambridge A-Levels after the Apolytirio
//    Gymnasiou. Use prominently — it is the Sweden/Czech wedge with
//    Athens-grade exam infrastructure behind it.
// 3. INTERNATIONAL-SCHOOL FAMILIES — supplements, waitlist bridges,
//    and A-Level continuation alongside Athens's and Thessaloniki's
//    established campuses.
// EXAM INFRASTRUCTURE: genuinely good — Athens's British-curriculum
// schools (Campion and peers run A-Levels) and British Council
// provision serve private candidates; hedge as always: "confirmed
// per family per session."
// UNIVERSITIES: Greek public universities admit via the Panhellenic
// examinations in Greek; international-track students target UCAS /
// Common Application / EU destinations, and since the 2024 law
// non-state branch campuses of foreign universities have begun
// opening in Athens — mention hedged. A small English-taught offer
// for international students exists — hedge.
// TIMEZONE: EET (UTC+2) / EEST (UTC+3 summer) — 0-1 hours behind
// Nairobi EAT; in summer Athens and Nairobi share the same clock.
